{"cuisine/cali-mex-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cali-mex-cuisine","n":"Cali-Mex cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Mexican-American cuisine","cuisine/mexican-american-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in United States of America","intro":[],"src":null,"wiki":"Cali-Mex cuisine","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cali-mex-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cali-Mex_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Mexican-American cuisine","cuisine/mexican-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Fast and Fresh Cal-Mex Cooking: West Coast-Inspired Dinners in 30 Minutes or Less","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Caitlin Prettyman","note":"Quick Cal-Mex weeknight dishes.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9798890030306","suggested":true,"isbn":"9798890030306"}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Mexican-American cuisine","cuisine/mexican-american-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"cuisine/south-korean-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/south-korean-cuisine","n":"South Korean cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in South Korea","intro":[["South Korea is a country in East Asia constituting the southern part of the Korean Peninsula. It is bordered to the north by North Korea, and the two countries are separated by the Korean Demilitarized Zone. Some dishes are shared by the two Koreas."],["Historically, Korean cuisine has evolved through centuries of social and political change. Originating from ancient agricultural and nomadic traditions in southern Manchuria and the Korean Peninsula, it has gone through a complex interaction of the natural environment and different cultural trends. Rice dishes and kimchi are staple Korean foods. In a traditional meal, they accompany both side dishes (banchan) and main courses like juk, bulgogi or noodles. Soju liquor is the best-known traditional Korean spirit."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"south-korean-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Korean_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":17,"links":[["Chalbori-ppang","dish/chalbori-ppang"],["Chimaek","dish/chimaek"],["Croffle","dish/croffle"],["Dak-galbi","dish/dak-galbi"],["Gilgeori toast","dish/gilgeori-toast"],["Hodu-gwaja","dish/hodu-gwaja"],["Hwangnam-ppang","dish/hwangnam-ppang"],["Jinju bibimbap","dish/jinju-bibimbap"],["Jjolmyeon","dish/jjolmyeon"],["Kkulppang","dish/kkulppang"],["Milmyeon","dish/milmyeon"],["Rabokki","dish/rabokki"],["Ramyeon","dish/ramyeon"],["So-tteok-so-tteok","dish/so-tteok-so-tteok"],["Tornado potato","dish/tornado-potato"],["Ttongppang","dish/ttongppang"],["Yangnyeom chicken","dish/yangnyeom-chicken"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Korean Bapsang","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"koreanbapsang.com","note":"Home-style Korean recipes from Hyosun, a Korean-American mom.","url":"https://www.koreanbapsang.com/category/recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"The Korean Cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Junghyun Park and Jungyoon Choi, 2023","note":"Large Phaidon collection of Korean recipes by chef Junghyun Park and Jungyoon Choi.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781838667542","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781838667542"}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Red bean paste","ingredient/red-bean-paste"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Walnut","ingredient/walnut"],["Noodle","ingredient/noodle"],["Noodles","ingredient/noodles"],["Tteok","ingredient/tteok"],["Glutinous barley","ingredient/glutinous-barley"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Beer","ingredient/beer"],["Korean fried chicken","ingredient/korean-fried-chicken"],["Croissant","ingredient/croissant"],["Waffle","ingredient/waffle"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Ketchup","ingredient/ketchup"],["Pan loaf","ingredient/pan-loaf"],["Scallion","ingredient/scallion"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Corn starch","ingredient/corn-starch"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Gochujang","ingredient/gochujang"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/5/5e/South_Korean_sides.jpg/500px-South_Korean_sides.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Douglas Paul Perkins","lic":"CC BY 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:South_Korean_sides.jpg"}},"cuisine/cuisine-of-the-mariana-islands":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-the-mariana-islands","n":"Mariana Islands cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in United States of America","intro":[["The cuisine of the Mariana Islands is largely made up of meat dishes, including pork, poultry, and meat from other land mammals. Some popular land animals consumed include Mariana fruit bat (fanihi in Chamorro). Guam and the Northern Marianas split in 1899, when Spain transferred Guam to the United States but the northern islands to Germany (later occupied by Japan), and so there are many similarities, especially the Chamorro food culture."],["Like in many other archipelagos, the islands' surrounding waters make seafood another popular option. Some seafoods include sea cucumbers and various fish. It is said that the Mariana Islands’ cuisine is heavily influenced by its neighbors’ Papuan, Hawaiian, and American cuisines. The Marianas’ cuisine is international, with many dishes, such as Korean kimchi, Filipino pancit and Spanish empanadas being enjoyed on the islands."],["The food of Guam and the Marianas is closely related, because it is linked by the traditions of the Chamorro people that inhabit this island chain. The northern Marianas had additional German, Japanese, and Carolinas’ influences, although in modern times the region is also influenced by Filipino, American, and Japanese food cultures. The core island culture is heavily influenced by what is available to eat, especially marine and island flora and fauna, combined with traditions of people and history. A good example of this is the donni’ såli chili pepper, which was brought centuries ago by sailing ships from the Americas, and is important for  fina'denni' sauce."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-the-mariana-islands"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_the_Mariana_Islands"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Annie's Chamorro Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"annieschamorrokitchen.com","note":"Blog of Chamorro home recipes, the shared cuisine of Guam and the Northern Mariana Islands.","url":"https://www.annieschamorrokitchen.com/?p=5054","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Within Mariana Islands cooking","count":1,"links":[["Guamanian cuisine","cuisine/culture-of-guam-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/f/fe/Kelaguen_1534_%2814529937155%29.jpg/500px-Kelaguen_1534_%2814529937155%29.jpg","w":500,"h":334,"by":"photosbybonbon671","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kelaguen_1534_(14529937155).jpg"}},"dish/aparon":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/aparon","n":"Aparon","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 3 cuisines","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Aparon (from apa, “wafer\" and chicharrón, \"crackling\") is a Filipino dessert made from toasted unconsecrated hostia (communion wafers) drizzled with caramelized sugar and, optionally, sesame seeds."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-aparon"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aparon"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Filipino cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine"],["Mindanao cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine-mindanao"],["Visayan cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine-visayas"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Panlasang Pinoy","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"panlasangpinoy.com","note":"Vanjo Merano's large library of Filipino recipes and cooking videos.","url":"https://panlasangpinoy.com/filipino-food/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Kawaling Pinoy","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"kawalingpinoy.com","note":"Lalaine Manalo's blog of Filipino home cooking.","url":"https://www.kawalingpinoy.com/about/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Flavours of Iloilo","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"flavoursofiloilo.blogspot.com","note":"Blog of Ilonggo home dishes from Iloilo in the Western Visayas.","url":"https://flavoursofiloilo.blogspot.com/2013/05/how-about-some-chicken-binakol.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Filipino cuisine","links":[["Adobo (Filipino dish)","dish/adobo-filipino-dish"],["Banana ketchup","dish/banana-ketchup"],["Champorado","dish/champorado"],["Filipino barbecue","dish/filipino-barbecue"],["Food for the gods","dish/food-for-the-gods"],["Isaw","dish/isaw"],["Kadyos, baboy, kag langka","dish/kadyos-baboy-kag-langka"],["Kadyos, manok, kag ubad","dish/kadyos-manok-kag-ubad"],["Kumukunsi","dish/kumukunsi"],["Mango float","dish/mango-float"],["Palapa (condiment)","dish/palapa-condiment"],["Pastil","dish/pastil"],["Piaparan","dish/piaparan"],["Pilipit","dish/pilipit"],["Piyanggang manok","dish/piyanggang-manok"],["Sushi bake","dish/sushi-bake"],["Tortang talong","dish/tortang-talong"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/0/0e/Aparon_wafers_%28Philippines%29.jpg/500px-Aparon_wafers_%28Philippines%29.jpg","w":500,"h":419,"by":"Judgefloro","lic":"CC0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aparon_wafers_(Philippines).jpg"}},"ingredient/double-cream":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/double-cream","n":"Double cream","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Cream is a dairy product composed of the higher-fat layer skimmed from the top of milk before homogenization. The butterfat, which is less dense, rises to the top and is skimmed off, resulting in gravity cream. The industrial production of cream instead uses centrifugal separators to make separator cream. Cream is often sold in grades defined by their butterfat content. It contains high levels of saturated fat, in ascending proportion from light cream to heavy cream."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-double-cream"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Double_cream"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Portuguese cuisine","cuisine/portuguese-cuisine",[["Bacalhau com natas","dish/bacalhau-com-natas"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Bacalhau","ingredient/bacalhau"],["Cream","ingredient/cream"],["Fried potatoes","ingredient/fried-potatoes"],["Fries","ingredient/fries"],["Onions","ingredient/onions"],["Salt cod","ingredient/salt-cod"]]}]},"ingredient/noodles":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/noodles","n":"Noodles","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 23 dishes across 19 cuisines","intro":[["Noodles are a type of food typically made from unleavened dough which is rolled flat and cut, stretched, or extruded into long strips or strings. Noodles are a staple food in many cultures and made into a variety of shapes. The most common noodles are those derived from Chinese cuisine or Italian cuisine. Italian noodles are generally referred to as pasta. In Chinese cuisine, the overarching term for noodles is 麵 (miàn in Mandarin), which refers specifically to dough-based noodles made from wheat or other grain-based dough. Chinese noodles also include another category, called 粉 (fěn), which are not made by kneading dough but from a starch slurry, such as rice noodles (mǐfěn, 米粉), and cellophane noodles (fěnsī, 粉丝 / 粉絲). These are not made from wheat dough, but are still regarded as noodles in English due to their physical form and culinary role."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-noodles"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Noodles"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":19,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Mie aceh","dish/mie-aceh"],["Mie ayam","dish/mie-ayam"],["Mie celor","dish/mie-celor"],["Mie gomak","dish/mie-gomak"],["Mie kocok","dish/mie-kocok"],["Pempek","dish/pempek"],["Tahu campur","dish/tahu-campur"]]],["Ashkenazi Jewish cuisine","cuisine/ashkenazi-jewish-cuisine",[["Kugel","dish/kugel"],["Yerushalmi Kugel","dish/yerushalmi-kugel"]]],["Kazakh cuisine","cuisine/kazakh-cuisine",[["Beshbarmak","dish/beshbarmak"],["Naryn (dish)","dish/naryn-dish"]]],["Korean Chinese cuisine","cuisine/korean-chinese-cuisine",[["Jjajangmyeon","dish/jjajangmyeon"],["Ulmyeon","dish/ulmyeon"]]],["Kyrgyz cuisine","cuisine/kyrgyz-cuisine",[["Beshbarmak","dish/beshbarmak"],["Naryn (dish)","dish/naryn-dish"]]],["South Korean cuisine","cuisine/south-korean-cuisine",[["Jjolmyeon","dish/jjolmyeon"],["Milmyeon","dish/milmyeon"]]],["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine",[["Pad see ew","dish/pad-see-ew"],["Rad naa","dish/rad-naa"]]],["Uzbek cuisine","cuisine/uzbek-cuisine",[["Beshbarmak","dish/beshbarmak"],["Naryn (dish)","dish/naryn-dish"]]],["Acehnese cuisine","cuisine/acehnese-cuisine",[["Mie aceh","dish/mie-aceh"]]],["Bashkir cuisine","cuisine/bashkir-cuisine",[["Beshbarmak","dish/beshbarmak"]]],["Brazilian cuisine","cuisine/brazilian-cuisine",[["Sobá","dish/soba"]]],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",[["Dandan noodles","dish/dandan-noodles"]]],["English cuisine","cuisine/english-cuisine",[["Windsor soup","dish/windsor-soup"]]],["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine",[["Ramen","dish/ramen"]]],["Palembang cuisine","cuisine/palembang-cuisine",[["Pempek","dish/pempek"]]],["Swiss cuisine","cuisine/swiss-cuisine",[["Älplermagronen","dish/alplermagronen"]]],["Taiwan cuisine","cuisine/taiwan-cuisine",[["Tàⁿ-á-mī","dish/ta-a-mi"]]],["Tatar cuisine","cuisine/tatar-cuisine",[["Beshbarmak","dish/beshbarmak"]]],["Turkmen cuisine","cuisine/turkmen-cuisine",[["Beshbarmak","dish/beshbarmak"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Noodle","ingredient/noodle"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Scallion","ingredient/scallion"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Mung bean sprout","ingredient/mung-bean-sprout"],["Shallot","ingredient/shallot"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Celery","ingredient/celery"],["Shrimp","ingredient/shrimp"],["Turmeric","ingredient/turmeric"]]}]},"ingredient/vienna-beef":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/vienna-beef","n":"Vienna Beef","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Vienna Beef Inc. is the main manufacturer of the hot dog used in the classic Chicago-style hot dog, as well as Polish sausage and Italian beef, delicacies of independent Chicago-style hot dog and beef stands. The company also produces a variety of deli meats, some of which are available at Chicago area supermarkets."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-vienna-beef"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vienna_Beef"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Chicago-style hot dog","dish/chicago-style-hot-dog"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Celery salt","ingredient/celery-salt"],["Chicago-style relish","ingredient/chicago-style-relish"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Hot dog","ingredient/hot-dog"],["Kosher dill pickle","ingredient/kosher-dill-pickle"],["Mustard","ingredient/mustard"],["Mustard (condiment)","ingredient/mustard-condiment"],["Pickled cucumber","ingredient/pickled-cucumber"],["Poppy seed","ingredient/poppy-seed"],["Sport peppers","ingredient/sport-peppers"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Vienna sausage","ingredient/vienna-sausage"],["White onion","ingredient/white-onion"]]}]},"dish/malbi":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/malbi","n":"Malbi","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Palembang cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Malbi is a meat stew originating from Palembang that resembles beef semur. This dish is dark in color, like rendang, and has a sweet and savory taste."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-malbi"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malbi"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Palembang cuisine","cuisine/palembang-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Sweet soy sauce","ingredient/sweet-soy-sauce"]]},{"h":"More from Palembang cuisine","links":[["Pempek","dish/pempek"],["Tekwan","dish/tekwan"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/6/6d/Malbi_dish%2C_Rumah_Makan_Sarinande_-_Kota_Jambi%2C_JA_%2816_Feb._2023%29.jpg/500px-Malbi_dish%2C_Rumah_Makan_Sarinande_-_Kota_Jambi%2C_JA_%2816_Feb._2023%29.jpg","w":500,"h":282,"by":"Firzafp","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malbi_dish%2C_Rumah_Makan_Sarinande_-_Kota_Jambi%2C_JA_(16_Feb._2023).jpg"}},"dish/mississippi-mud-pie":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/mississippi-mud-pie","n":"Mississippi mud pie","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Southern American cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Mississippi mud pie is a chocolate-based dessert pie that is likely to have originated in the U.S. state of Mississippi, hence the name. It typically contains a gooey chocolate sauce, brownie and chocolate custard on top of a crumbly chocolate crust. It is usually served with ice cream, but can also be served alongside whipped cream, boiled custard, or vanilla yogurt. Its name is due to its resemblance to mud."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-mississippi-mud-pie"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mississippi_mud_pie"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Southern American cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-southern-united-states"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Chocolate","ingredient/chocolate"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Lana's Cooking","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"lanascooking.com","note":"Lana Stuart's blog of Southern home cooking from Georgia.","url":"https://www.lanascooking.com/about/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"A Southern Soul: Southern Classics","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"asouthernsoul.com","note":"Collection of classic Southern recipes on a Southern home cooking blog.","url":"https://asouthernsoul.com/recipes/southern-classics","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Southern American cuisine","links":[["Ambrosia (fruit salad)","dish/ambrosia-fruit-salad"],["Barbecue bologna","dish/barbecue-bologna"],["Chess pie","dish/chess-pie"],["Chicken Maryland","dish/chicken-maryland"],["Chitlins","dish/chitlins"],["Conecuh Sausage","dish/conecuh-sausage"],["Grape hull pie","dish/grape-hull-pie"],["Hash (stew)","dish/hash-stew"],["Lemon pepper wings","dish/lemon-pepper-wings"],["Monroe Sausage","dish/monroe-sausage"],["Moon Pie","dish/moon-pie"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/6/6a/Mud_pie_%28cropped%29.JPG/500px-Mud_pie_%28cropped%29.JPG","w":500,"h":349,"by":"Mud_pie.JPG: User:Chensiyuan derivative work: Mindmatrix","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mud_pie_(cropped).JPG"}},"dish/oatmeal-ball":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/oatmeal-ball","n":"Oatmeal ball","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Swedish cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["The oatmeal ball or the chocolate ball (Swedish: chokladboll) is a type of unbaked pastry that is a popular Danish and Swedish confectionery."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-oatmeal-ball"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oatmeal_ball"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Swedish cuisine","cuisine/swedish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":7,"links":[["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Cocoa powder","ingredient/cocoa-powder"],["Coffee","ingredient/coffee"],["Grated coconut","ingredient/grated-coconut"],["Oatmeal","ingredient/oatmeal"],["Rolled oats","ingredient/rolled-oats"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Scandicuisine: Swedish recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"scandicuisine.com","note":"Traditional Swedish recipes from Cecilia, a cook from a Finnish-Swedish family on Åland.","url":"https://scandicuisine.com/category/swedish-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Swedish cuisine","links":[["Biff à la Lindström","dish/biff-a-la-lindstrom"],["Blåbärssoppa","dish/blabarssoppa"],["Blodpalt","dish/blodpalt"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Flying Jacob","dish/flying-jacob"],["Fruktsoppa","dish/fruktsoppa"],["Glögg","dish/glogg"],["Hasselback potatoes","dish/hasselback-potatoes"],["Isterband","dish/isterband"],["Kebab pizza","dish/kebab-pizza"],["Korv stroganoff","dish/korv-stroganoff"],["Kroppkaka","dish/kroppkaka"],["Limpa","dish/limpa"],["Lutefisk","dish/lutefisk"],["Ostkaka","dish/ostkaka"],["Rødgrød","dish/r-dgr-d"],["Semla","dish/semla"],["Smörgåstårta","dish/smorgastarta"],["Strong beer","dish/strong-beer"],["Surströmming","dish/surstromming"],["Swedish meatballs","dish/swedish-meatballs"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/2/2c/Chokladbollar.jpg/500px-Chokladbollar.jpg","w":500,"h":344,"by":"No machine-readable author provided. Stefan w~commonswiki assumed (based on copyright…","lic":"CC BY 2.5","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.5","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chokladbollar.jpg"}},"ingredient/ram":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/ram","n":"Ram","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Sheep or domestic sheep  (Ovis aries) are a domesticated ruminant mammal typically kept as livestock. Although the term sheep can apply to other species in the genus Ovis, in everyday usage it almost always refers to domesticated sheep. Like all ruminants, sheep are members of the order Artiodactyla, the even-toed ungulates. There are around 1.3 billion domestic sheep as of 2021, making them easily the most common species of sheep. An adult female is referred to as a ewe ( yoo); an intact male as a ram, occasionally a tup; a castrated male as a wether; and a young sheep as a lamb."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-ram"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ram_(sheep)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Nigerian cuisine","cuisine/nigerian-cuisine",[["Suya","dish/suya"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Cayenne pepper","ingredient/cayenne-pepper"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Goat","ingredient/goat"],["Shrimp","ingredient/shrimp"],["Tripe","ingredient/tripe"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/2/2c/Flock_of_sheep.jpg/500px-Flock_of_sheep.jpg","w":500,"h":760,"by":"Keith Weller","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flock_of_sheep.jpg"}},"ingredient/leftovers":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/leftovers","n":"Leftovers","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Leftovers are foods remaining unconsumed at the end of a meal, which may be saved for eating later. Inedible remains like bones are considered waste, not leftovers. Depending on the situation, the amount of food, and the type of food, leftovers may be saved or thrown away. The use of leftovers depends on where the meal was eaten, the preferences of the diner, and the local culture.  Leftovers from meals at home are often eaten later. This is facilitated by the private environment and convenience of airtight containers and refrigeration. People may eat leftovers directly from the refrigerator, reheat them, or use them as ingredients to make a new dish. At restaurants, uneaten food from meals is sometimes taken by diners for later consumption."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-leftovers"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leftovers"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",[["Tshài-bué-thng","dish/tshai-bue-thng"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Brassica juncea","ingredient/brassica-juncea"],["Garcinia atroviridis","ingredient/garcinia-atroviridis"]]}],"img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/0/0b/Leftovers_from_Thanksgiving.jpg/500px-Leftovers_from_Thanksgiving.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Andrew Nash from Vienna, Austria","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leftovers_from_Thanksgiving.jpg"}}}